our title:Evolution of social media platforms (MySpace to
Facebook/Instagram/TikTok)

First viewpoint analysis:
With the evolution of social media came with impacts on how companies run their businesses and how society functions around the usage of social media.
For businesses, social media brought new ways to advertise and sell products for customers to buy. On the societal aspect, it was able to allow
people new ways to communicate with each other, as well as making it easier to talk to other people from around the globe. Right now, we are going to be looking at the business
aspect of the evolution of social media, to see how companies were able to adapt to the ever changing land scape of social media business.

A way that major brands were able to bring more attention to their business was through advertisements on social media platforms. Advertisements on social
media was introduced as early as 2006 with Facebook, after that was Twitter enabling ads in 2010, then other sites such as Snapchat, Youtube and more adding
ads to their platforms. Companies saw this as an opportunity to make their brands more popular, so they started to use social media as a way to grow. So they started
putting ads on these sites either in picture or video form to inform people about their products, or recruit them for their company. Even making the some of the ads
entaining to some degree to entice people. Other social media sites even created accounts on their competitors websites to advertise and talk about upcoming updates
to their own platforms.

According to Yoesoep Edhie Rachmad on his book 'Like, Share, Sell: Social Media for the Aspiring Business Owner,' he expalins how the rise of social media has
given businesses the ability to look at real time data of their costumers to get a good idea of topics such as "engagment rates, follower demographics, and
content reach" to truly understand their audience and use that data to help improve aspects of their company to bring more success to the business as
a whole. Despite these features being able to massively help business owners, including some on the smaller side of the spectrum, he does warn to not put too
muchreliance on quantitive metrics. These sets of data do not capture the heart and soul of human connection. Which he argues that the success of social media mainly
comes from the ability to listen, repond, and adapt in ways that corresponds to the ever changing interests or demands of their audience.
